---
import Doc from "../../layouts/Doc.astro";

/**
 * How to point each client at a project.
 *
 * Kept concrete rather than "add the URL in your client's settings", because
 * every one of these puts it somewhere different and the difference is the
 * whole reason someone opens this page.
 */
---

<Doc
  title="Connecting a client"
  description="Where to paste the MCP URL in Claude, ChatGPT, Cursor, VS Code, Copilot, Devin, Zed and every other MCP client."
  canonicalPath="/docs/clients/"
>
  <p>
    There are two URLs to choose between, and every client below takes either one. Both sign in
    through your browser the first time they are used, and nothing else has to be configured on
    either side.
  </p>

  <h2 id="which-url">Which URL</h2>

  <p>
    <code>https://exeora.dev/mcp</code> is the same for everyone and covers several projects at once.
    When you authorize a client on it, the consent screen asks what it may reach: all your projects,
    including the ones you add later, or only the ones you tick. The client uses{" "}
    <code>list_projects</code> to see them and names the project on each tool call whenever more than
    one is available.
  </p>

  <p>
    With all projects chosen, you add the URL once and it keeps working as projects come and go: a
    project is given to that client the moment it is created, from the dashboard or from a
    machine. With a chosen list, a project you add later stays out of reach until you give it to
    the client, either while adding the project in the dashboard or under <strong>Clients</strong>.
    Taking one project away from a client that had all of them turns it into a chosen list.
  </p>

  <p>
    Either way, what a client is given is a project, not a machine. It reaches that project in
    every location it lives in, and <code>list_projects</code> shows it those locations and the
    state of each.
  </p>

  <p>
    <code>https://exeora.dev/p/&lt;project&gt;/mcp</code>, which <code>exeora project add</code> prints,
    is one project and nothing else. The project is in the URL rather than in anything the client
    sends, so a client on it has no way to name another project: the separation is structural rather
    than something the model is asked to respect. That is the stronger guarantee, and the cost is one
    entry per project in every client.
  </p>

  <p>
    Ticking a single project on the account URL gives you the same reach as a project URL, and you
    can change which projects a client reaches, or cut it off entirely, from the dashboard under
    Clients. The two are separate consents: a client authorized both ways keeps whichever you do not
    take away.
  </p>

  <p>
    The steps below use <code>https://exeora.dev/mcp</code>; a project URL goes in the same place.
    Menus move between releases, so each section names the setting as the client's own
    documentation does today.
  </p>

  <h2 id="claude-code">Claude Code</h2>

  <pre>claude mcp add --transport http exeora https://exeora.dev/mcp</pre>

  <p>
    Then run <code>/mcp</code> inside Claude Code, or <code>claude mcp login exeora</code>, to sign
    in through the browser. Add <code>--scope project</code> to share the entry through the
    repository's <code>.mcp.json</code>, or <code>--scope user</code> for every project.
  </p>

  <h2 id="claude">Claude</h2>

  <p>
    Customize → Connectors → <strong>+</strong> → Add custom connector, paste the URL and choose
    Add, then Connect. On Team and Enterprise an owner adds it first, under Organization settings →
    Connectors, and members connect to it from Customize. The free plan allows one custom
    connector.
  </p>

  <p>
    Claude speaks the 2025 protocol today, which matters only for confirmation prompts: see{" "}
    <a href="/docs/policy/#confirming">confirming a change</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="chatgpt">ChatGPT</h2>

  <p>
    Custom MCP servers are part of developer mode, on the web, on Plus, Pro, Business, Enterprise and
    Education. Turn it on under Settings → Security and login → Developer mode, then open{" "}
    <a href="https://chatgpt.com/plugins">chatgpt.com/plugins</a>, add an app with the URL, and
    choose OAuth. In a chat, pick it from the <strong>+</strong> menu under Developer mode. On
    Business and Enterprise a workspace admin may have to allow developer mode first.
  </p>

  <p>Same note about the protocol revision as Claude.</p>

  <h2 id="cursor">Cursor</h2>

  <p>
    Add the server from Cursor's MCP settings, or write it to <code>~/.cursor/mcp.json</code> for
    every project or <code>.cursor/mcp.json</code> for one:
  </p>

  <pre>{`{
  "mcpServers": {
    "exeora": { "url": "https://exeora.dev/mcp" }
  }
}`}</pre>

  <p>Cursor registers itself and opens the sign-in when the server is first used.</p>

  <h2 id="vscode">VS Code</h2>

  <p>
    Run <strong>MCP: Add Server</strong> from the command palette, choose HTTP, paste the URL, and
    pick Workspace or Global. The same entry in <code>.vscode/mcp.json</code>:
  </p>

  <pre>{`{
  "servers": {
    "exeora": { "type": "http", "url": "https://exeora.dev/mcp" }
  }
}`}</pre>

  <h2 id="copilot">GitHub Copilot</h2>

  <p>
    <strong>In VS Code</strong>, Copilot's agent mode uses the servers above: add Exeora as in{" "}
    <a href="#vscode">VS Code</a> and sign in from the <strong>Auth</strong> action shown over the
    entry. <strong>In JetBrains IDEs</strong>, open Copilot Chat in agent mode, choose the tools
    icon, then Add MCP Tools, and add the server to the <code>mcp.json</code> it opens; OAuth there
    is still in preview.
  </p>

  <pre>{`{
  "servers": {
    "exeora": { "url": "https://exeora.dev/mcp" }
  }
}`}</pre>

  <p>
    On Copilot Business and Enterprise, an administrator has to enable the{" "}
    <strong>MCP servers in Copilot</strong> policy first; it is off by default. Copilot's cloud
    agent on github.com does not support servers that sign in with OAuth, so it cannot use Exeora
    today.
  </p>

  <h2 id="devin">Devin</h2>

  <p>
    Customize → MCPs → Add MCP → Add custom MCP. Choose the HTTP transport, paste the URL, set the
    authentication method to OAuth, save, and choose <strong>Connect</strong>. Organization access
    shares one connection with everyone; Personal has each member sign in as themselves, which is
    usually what you want, since the grant is to your Exeora account. Adding a server takes the
    Manage MCP Servers permission.
  </p>

  <h2 id="antigravity">Antigravity</h2>

  <p>
    From the <strong>…</strong> menu at the top of the agent panel, open MCP Servers → Manage MCP
    Servers → View raw config, and add the server to <code>mcp_config.json</code>. Antigravity
    reads the address from <code>serverUrl</code>; <code>url</code> is not supported.
  </p>

  <pre>{`{
  "mcpServers": {
    "exeora": { "serverUrl": "https://exeora.dev/mcp" }
  }
}`}</pre>

  <p>Then choose <strong>Authenticate</strong> for it under Agent Settings → Customizations.</p>

  <h2 id="amp">Amp</h2>

  <pre>amp mcp add exeora https://exeora.dev/mcp</pre>

  <p>
    Or in <code>~/.config/amp/settings.json</code>. Amp registers itself and opens the browser to
    sign in the next time it starts.
  </p>

  <pre>{`{
  "amp.mcpServers": {
    "exeora": { "url": "https://exeora.dev/mcp" }
  }
}`}</pre>

  <h2 id="zed">Zed</h2>

  <p>
    Settings → AI → MCP Servers → Add Server → Add Remote Server, or in <code>settings.json</code>.
    With no <code>Authorization</code> header configured, Zed asks you to sign in with OAuth.
  </p>

  <pre>{`{
  "context_servers": {
    "exeora": { "url": "https://exeora.dev/mcp" }
  }
}`}</pre>

  <h2 id="opencode">OpenCode</h2>

  <p>In <code>opencode.json</code>:</p>

  <pre>{`{
  "$schema": "https://opencode.ai/config.json",
  "mcp": {
    "exeora": { "type": "remote", "url": "https://exeora.dev/mcp", "enabled": true }
  }
}`}</pre>

  <p>
    OpenCode signs in by itself on first use; <code>opencode mcp auth exeora</code> starts the
    sign-in by hand.
  </p>

  <h2 id="grok">Grok</h2>

  <p>
    On grok.com, open Connectors → New Connector → Custom, paste the URL and complete the sign-in.
    On Business and Enterprise a team admin adds the connector first. xAI's documentation does not
    yet say whether custom connectors register themselves with OAuth; if the sign-in never
    completes, that is the part missing on Grok's side, and nothing on Exeora's can change it. The
    Grok API's remote MCP tool takes only a bearer token, so it cannot sign in to Exeora.
  </p>

  <h2 id="perplexity">Perplexity</h2>

  <p>
    Account Settings → Connectors → Custom connector → Remote. Paste the URL, choose OAuth for
    authentication and Streamable HTTP for transport. Custom connectors are on the Pro, Max and
    Enterprise plans, and on Enterprise an admin has to allow members to add them.
  </p>

  <h2 id="more-clients">More clients</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Codex</strong>: <code>codex mcp add exeora --url https://exeora.dev/mcp</code>, then{" "}
      <code>codex mcp login exeora</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Gemini CLI</strong>:{" "}
      <code>gemini mcp add --transport http exeora https://exeora.dev/mcp</code>, then{" "}
      <code>/mcp auth exeora</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Windsurf</strong>: add the server to Cascade's <code>mcp_config.json</code> with{" "}
      <code>serverUrl</code>, as for Antigravity.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="anything-else">Anything else</h2>

  <p>
    Any client that speaks MCP over Streamable HTTP with OAuth 2.1 will work; there is nothing
    Exeora-specific to implement. To try one by hand:
  </p>

  <pre>bunx @modelcontextprotocol/inspector@2.1.0</pre>

  <h2 id="call-fails">If a call fails immediately</h2>

  <p>
    <code>LOCAL_EXECUTOR_OFFLINE</code> means a machine of yours is off, or{" "}
    <code>exeora connect</code> is not running on it: the machine that holds the workspace the call
    named, or the default location when it named none. Nothing is queued, on purpose.{" "}
    <code>EXECUTOR_WAKING</code> means an instance on Exeora Cloud was asleep and did not come up in
    time, or the instance of the project root is being made, and the call is worth sending again.
    See{" "}
    <a href="/docs/troubleshooting/">troubleshooting</a>.
  </p>
</Doc>
